    title: "Read the Candle",
    category: "Candlesticks",
    minutes: 6,
    summary: "Four prices describe the entire contest between buyers and sellers.",
    demo: "candleAnatomy",
    featured: true,
    content: "# Anatomy of a Candle\n\nEvery candle on your Nifty or stock chart is a story of one time period — told in four numbers: **Open, High, Low, Close (OHLC)**.\n\nLearn to read those four numbers, and a chart stops being noise.\n\n---\n\n## The Four Parts\n\n| Part | What it is | What it tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Open** | First traded price of the period | Where the period started |\n| **High** | Highest price touched | How far buyers pushed |\n| **Low** | Lowest price touched | How far sellers pushed |\n| **Close** | Last traded price | Where the period ended relative to its open and range |\n\nVisually, these become two elements:\n\n**1. The Real Body** — the thick rectangle between Open and Close.\n- Green / hollow body → Close above Open (buyers won)\n- Red / filled body → Close below Open (sellers won)\n\n**2. The Wicks (Shadows)** — the thin lines above and below the body.\n- **Upper wick** = the distance from the body top to the High — price went up there and got rejected\n- **Lower wick** = the distance from the body bottom to the Low — price went down there and got bought back\n\n> A wick is not just a line. It is a **failed attempt**. That is why wicks often matter more than bodies.\n\n---\n\n## Reading Body Size\n\n| Body | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Long body** | Strong conviction. One side dominated the whole session. |\n| **Small body** | Indecision. Both sides fought to a draw. |\n| **No body (Doji)** | Open ≈ Close. Perfect stalemate — often a warning before a turn. |\n| **No wicks (Marubozu)** | Total control from bell to bell. Very strong signal. |\n\n---\n\n## Reading Wick Position\n\nThese features describe the candle; whether they help a strategy needs testing:\n\n- **Long lower wick, small body on top** — sellers pushed price down, buyers slammed it back up. Demand showed up. (Hammer shape)\n- **Long upper wick, small body at bottom** — buyers pushed price up, sellers crushed it back down. Supply showed up. (Shooting star shape)\n- **Wicks on both sides** — a tug of war. Wait for the next candle.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** the longer the wick, the stronger the rejection at that price. Mark that level — it often becomes support or resistance later.\n\n---\n\n## The Indian Market Context\n\nA few things that make our candles behave differently from a 24-hour market:\n\n**Check the session behind the candle.** For NSE equities, an opening price discovered in the regular pre-open auction becomes the daily Open. If no price is discovered, the first normal-market trade sets it. Exchange, instrument and special-session rules can differ.\n\n**Gaps are common.** Since NSE/BSE close overnight, news, global cues (SGX Nifty / Gift Nifty, US markets) and results get absorbed at the Open. This produces gap-up and gap-down candles with no wick on one side — normal here, rare in forex or crypto.\n\n**Circuit limits distort candles.** A stock locked in a 5%, 10% or 20% upper circuit prints a body with no upper wick and almost no volume behind it. Do not read that as \"strength\" the same way.\n\n**Expiry days lie.** Weekly and monthly index-expiry sessions can produce long, whippy wicks driven by option positioning, not by real directional conviction. Treat expiry-day candles with suspicion.\n\n**Illiquid small caps need extra care.** A long wick on low volume may reflect a small number of trades. The candle alone does not reveal how many orders created it; check liquidity and volume.\n\n---\n\n## The 3-Question Checklist\n\nBefore you act on any candle, ask:\n\n1. **Where did it close within its own range?** Near the high = strength. Near the low = weakness. Middle = ignore it.\n2. **Where is it on the chart?** The exact same candle means opposite things at a support zone versus after a five-day rally. Location beats pattern.\n3. **What is the volume?** A big candle on thin volume is a rumour. A big candle on heavy volume is a decision.\n\n---\n\n## Common Beginner Mistakes\n\n- **Trading a single candle in isolation.** Candles are words; you need the sentence.\n- **Judging colour instead of close.** A red candle that closes near its high after a deep dip is actually bullish behaviour.\n- **Ignoring the timeframe.** A bearish 5-minute candle inside a strong daily uptrend is meaningless noise.\n- **Forgetting that a candle is still forming.** Everything changes at 3:30 PM. Do not act on a half-built daily candle at 11 AM.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- The **body** shows the outcome. The **wicks** show the struggle.\n- **Close is king** — it is the price both sides finally agreed on.\n- Candle + **location** + **volume** = a signal. Candle alone = a guess.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Markets carry risk — please consult a SEBI-registered advisor before investing.*",
    related: ["six-candlestick-patterns","support-resistance-zones","what-volume-tells-you"],
