    summary: "Forget the catalogue. These six patterns reveal the shifts in control that matter.",
    demo: "tape",
    content: "# Six Candlestick Patterns in Context\n\n### *Forty patterns is not knowledge. It is a memory test you will fail under pressure.*\n\nSearch \"candlestick patterns\" and you get a chart of forty-plus names — Three Black Crows, Abandoned Baby, Concealing Baby Swallow. This lesson focuses on a handful of patterns used to interpret **possible shifts in buying and selling pressure.** Their usefulness needs testing in context.\n\nHere are six. Learn their shapes, then examine their limits.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Hammer\n\nA small body at the top, a long lower wick at least twice the body, little or no upper wick.\n\n**What happened:** Sellers drove price down hard. Buyers absorbed everything and pushed it back to near the open.\n\n**Where it counts:** Only after a decline, at a support zone. A hammer in the middle of a range is decoration.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Shooting Star\n\nThe hammer upside down. Small body at the bottom, long upper wick, appearing after an advance.\n\n**What happened:** Buyers pushed price up and got overwhelmed. Supply appeared at that level.\n\n**Where it counts:** After a rally, at resistance or a prior high. The longer the upper wick, the more meaningful the rejection.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Bullish Engulfing\n\nA green candle whose body completely covers the previous red candle's body.\n\n**What happened:** One session erased the entire prior session's selling and then some. That is a genuine change of hands, not a drift.\n\n**Strength check:** Bigger engulfing body and higher volume than the candle it swallowed. Without volume, downgrade it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Bearish Engulfing\n\nThe mirror image at the top of a move. A red body swallowing the prior green body.\n\n**Where it counts most:** After an extended run, especially on a stock that has gone up for several sessions without pausing. This is often the first honest sign that the crowd has finished buying.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The Doji\n\nOpen and close are effectively the same. A cross, not a candle.\n\n**What happened:** Complete stalemate.\n\n**The nuance most people miss:** a doji is not bearish or bullish. It is a *pause*. Its meaning comes entirely from what precedes it and what follows. A doji after a trend may suggest hesitation; it does not establish that momentum has stopped. Inside a range, its significance also depends on context.\n\n**A doji alone is not an entry rule.** A later candle adds information, but can also give a false signal.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Marubozu\n\nA long body with virtually no wicks. Price opened at one extreme and closed at the other.\n\n**What happened:** One side controlled the entire session, start to finish. No meaningful counter-attack.\n\n**Why it matters:** A bullish marubozu breaking out of a range is one of the cleanest continuation signals available. It shows conviction rather than drift.\n\n**Indian caveat:** verify it is not a circuit-locked candle. A stock frozen at upper circuit prints a wickless body on almost no genuine two-way trade. That is not conviction — it is a queue.\n\n---\n\n## The Rule That Governs All Six\n\n**Read the pattern in its location. Neither guarantees the next move.**\n\nThe same hammer is:\n- A high-quality signal at a tested support level after a decline\n- Meaningless in the middle of a sideways chop\n- A trap in a stock in sustained downtrend with no support anywhere nearby\n\nBefore you name the pattern, answer three questions:\n\n1. **Where is it?** At a level that matters, or in no-man's-land?\n2. **What came before it?** A pattern only reverses something. If there is no prior move, there is nothing to reverse.\n3. **What is the volume?** Compare volume with recent activity. Volume alone cannot reveal conviction or trader intent.\n\n---\n\n## How to Actually Trade Them\n\n**Wait for confirmation.** A hammer is a hypothesis. The next candle closing above its high is the evidence. Entering on the hammer itself is guessing; waiting for confirmation changes the entry price and may filter some failed setups. Test that trade-off rather than assuming it improves results.\n\n**Use the pattern to define your stop.** The best thing about these six is not the entry — it is that each gives you a clear invalidation point. Below the hammer's low. Above the shooting star's high. If you cannot place a logical stop, you do not have a trade.\n\n**Ignore them on expiry day and in illiquid stocks.** Both produce patterns generated by mechanics rather than opinion.\n\n**Respect the higher timeframe.** A bearish engulfing on the 15-minute chart inside a strong daily uptrend is noise. Trade with the bigger picture or stand aside.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- The six patterns covered here are: **Hammer, Shooting Star, Bullish Engulfing, Bearish Engulfing, Doji, Marubozu.**\n- Each one describes a transfer of control, not a magic prediction.\n- **Location + volume + confirmation** provide context for a testable setup. They do not establish an edge or guarantee a profitable trade.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Markets carry risk — please consult a SEBI-registered advisor before investing.*",
