    content: "# Circuit Limits: How to Not Get Trapped\n\n### *A circuit is not a price. It is a locked door.*\n\nYour stock is up 20% and you cannot sell. Or it is down 10% and there is not a single buyer on the screen. Nothing is broken — you have just met India's circuit system, and most retail traders meet it for the first time on the wrong side.\n\n---\n\n## Two Different Things, Often Confused\n\n**Price bands (stock circuits)** limit how far *one stock* can move in a day.\n\n**Market-wide circuit breakers** halt *the entire market* when the index moves violently.\n\nThey run simultaneously and are governed by SEBI. Confusing one for the other is where the panic starts.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Stock Price Bands\n\nFor NSE cash securities subject to fixed daily bands, the published bands include **2%, 5%, 10% and 20%**. Securities with derivatives use an operating range instead; special cases and daily exchange notices must be checked separately.\n\nSmaller, more volatile and more manipulation-prone names get *tighter* bands. That is the point — the band is a leash, and the exchange decides the length.\n\n| Situation | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **Upper circuit** | Price ceiling for the day. Buy orders pile up, sellers vanish. |\n| **Lower circuit** | Price floor for the day. Sell orders pile up, buyers vanish. |\n\n**The critical detail:** hitting a circuit does not always stop trading. It stops *price movement*. You can still place an order — it simply may never find a counterparty.\n\n### Why Upper Circuit Is Not Good News\n\nA stock locked at upper circuit looks like the best thing you have ever seen. On the chart it prints a strong body with no upper wick.\n\nBut ask what created it. If it is a genuine earnings surprise or an order win, fine. If it is a 5%-band microcap with a Telegram group behind it and a thousand shares traded, that candle is not demand — it is a queue of retail buyers with nobody selling to them.\n\n**The trap:** you get filled near the top of a multi-day upper-circuit run, right as the operator finishes distributing. The next session opens at lower circuit and you cannot get out.\n\n### Why Lower Circuit Is Genuinely Dangerous\n\nThis is the one that damages accounts.\n\nAt lower circuit, your sell order sits in a queue behind thousands of others. **There is no exit at any price.** A stop-loss does not help — it triggers into a market with no bids. If selling pressure continues, the stock can hit lower circuit again the next day. And the next. Small caps have lost 40–50% over consecutive circuit-locked sessions with retail holders unable to sell a single share.\n\nThis is why **liquidity is a risk parameter, not a detail.**\n\n### The Exception: F&O Stocks\n\nStocks in the F&O segment do not carry fixed daily bands. They operate with **dynamic price bands** — an operating range that the exchange relaxes further as the stock moves. They can travel much further in a day. They remain subject to market-wide halts.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Market-Wide Circuit Breakers\n\nIntroduced by SEBI in 2001, these halt **all equity and equity-derivative trading nationwide**. They trigger on Nifty 50 or Sensex movement of **10%, 15% or 20%** from the previous close — whichever index breaches first — and the rule is symmetric for up moves and down moves.\n\n| Move | Time of trigger | Halt |\n|---|---|---|\n| **10%** | Before 1:00 PM | 45 minutes |\n| **10%** | At or after 1:00 PM, before 2:30 PM | 15 minutes |\n| **10%** | At or after 2:30 PM | No halt |\n| **15%** | Before 1:00 PM | 1 hour 45 minutes |\n| **15%** | At or after 1:00 PM, before 2:00 PM | 45 minutes |\n| **15%** | At or after 2:00 PM | Rest of the day |\n| **20%** | Any time | Rest of the day |\n\nWhere trading resumes that day after a 10% or 15% halt, a **15-minute pre-open call auction** precedes continuous trading.\n\n**Check the order book after a halt.** NSE's secondary-market FAQ states that outstanding orders are cancelled on an index-based market-wide circuit halt. This does not establish what happens during every kind of halt or to broker-held conditional orders; confirm the applicable notice and order status.\n\nIndia has seen this rarely — most memorably in March 2020 during the COVID crash. Rare, but not theoretical.\n\n---\n\n## How to Not Get Trapped\n\n**Check the band before you buy, not after.** It is on the NSE quote page. A 5% band on a stock you are planning to trade actively is a warning label.\n\n**Check average traded volume.** If a stock trades a few lakh rupees a day, you are not a trader in it — you are a hostage. Ask: can I sell my full position in one day without moving the price?\n\n**Never trade circuit-locked momentum on borrowed conviction.** If the reason you are buying is that it is *already* at upper circuit, you have no reason at all.\n\n**Treat consecutive circuit runs as distribution, not accumulation.** Five upper circuits in a row usually means someone is being sold to. Check who.\n\n**Size for the exit, not the entry.** In an illiquid name, assume you might be stuck for several sessions and position so that outcome is survivable.\n\n**Check order status after a halt.** Re-enter cancelled orders only after confirming what remains active.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- Price bands cap one stock. Circuit breakers halt the whole market.\n- A circuit stops the price, not the panic.\n- Upper circuit is a queue, not proof of value.\n- Lower circuits can prevent an exit; prior liquidity does not guarantee an available buyer.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Circuit rules and price bands are set by SEBI and the exchanges and can change — verify current limits on the NSE or BSE website before trading.*",
