    title: "Expiry Day",
    category: "Market Mechanics",
    minutes: 7,
    summary: "Theta, positioning and forced unwinds make expiry a different market.",
    demo: "probability",
    content: "# Expiry Day Is a Different Market\n\n### *On expiry day, price is not discovering value. It is settling a bill.*\n\nEvery week, one session behaves nothing like the others. Charts break. Support fails and instantly recovers. A stock or index grinds toward a round number and refuses to leave it. Nothing is wrong with your analysis — you are simply trading on a day when a different force is in charge.\n\n---\n\n## First, Know Which Day\n\nThe table distinguishes NSE contracts from BSE Sensex options. NSE specifications were checked on 12 November 2026; the BSE Thursday schedule is supported by its September 2025 transition notice. Confirm the live contract and holiday adjustments with the relevant exchange.\n\n| Contract | Weekly expiry | Monthly expiry |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nifty 50 (NSE)** | Every **Tuesday** | Last Tuesday |\n| **Sensex (BSE)** | Every **Thursday** | Last Thursday |\n| **Bank Nifty, FinNifty, Midcap Nifty** | **None** | Last Tuesday for monthly contracts; Bank Nifty options also have quarterly expiries |\n| **NSE single stock F&O** | None | Last Tuesday; physically settled |\n\nTwo things drove this. SEBI's circular effective **20 November 2024** limited each exchange to a single weekly index option, killing Bank Nifty, FinNifty, Bankex and Midcap weeklies. Then from **1 September 2025**, NSE and BSE swapped days — Nifty moved from Thursday to Tuesday, Sensex to Thursday — to spread the volume out.\n\n**If expiry day falls on a market holiday, expiry moves to the previous trading day.** Always confirm the live contract on the exchange site before you take a position.\n\nThe practical result: the week now has **two pressure points**, not one crowded Thursday.\n\n---\n\n## Why the Price Action Changes\n\nOn a normal day, price moves because people are forming opinions about value. On expiry day, price moves because **enormous option positions have to be settled** — and the people holding them have a direct financial interest in where the index lands.\n\nThree forces take over.\n\n### 1. Theta Collapse\n\nTime value is the fuel in an option's price. On expiry day, that fuel burns out in hours. Out-of-the-money options can lose most of their value in a single afternoon **even if the index moves in your favour** — because it did not move enough, fast enough.\n\nThis is why buying cheap OTM options on expiry day feels like a lottery ticket. It is priced like one too.\n\n### 2. IV Crush\n\nImplied volatility is the market's expectation of movement. As expiry approaches and uncertainty resolves, IV often drops sharply in the morning. Premiums deflate. Option *buyers* lose value from this alone; option *sellers* collect it.\n\n### 3. Pinning and Max Pain\n\n\"Max pain\" is the level at which the largest number of open contracts expire worthless. Some market participants observe that the index gravitates toward such levels near expiry, as large option writers defend the strikes they are short.\n\nWhether you accept that as causation or coincidence, the observable behaviour is real: **the index often gets magnetised to round strike levels and stops trending.** Breakouts fail. Ranges hold that should not hold.\n\n---\n\n## What This Means for Your Chart Reading\n\nThis is where expiry day quietly ruins technical analysis.\n\n- **Long wicks are not rejections.** They are option-driven whipsaws. A sharp spike and full retrace at 2:30 PM on expiry is positioning, not supply and demand.\n- **Breakouts have a poor strike rate.** Price escapes a level, then gets dragged back toward the pinned strike.\n- **Support and resistance behave inconsistently.** Levels break and un-break within minutes.\n- **The daily candle is often misleading.** Do not carry expiry-day highs and lows forward as meaningful levels without confirmation from a later session.\n\n**Rule:** on expiry day, weight your levels from *previous* sessions and treat that day's own structure as low quality data.\n\n---\n\n## If You Trade Expiry Day Anyway\n\nPlenty do. Activity can be high, but liquidity and movement vary by contract, strike and session. Just go in with the right assumptions.\n\n**Know your expiry day and your contract.** Confirm on the exchange site. Do not assume Thursday out of habit.\n\n**Understand you are trading a decay product.** As an option buyer you need direction *and* speed. As a seller you have a favourable clock and open-ended risk. Neither is free.\n\n**Define maximum loss before entry.** Not after.\n\n**Do not buy an option because the premium looks cheap.** Cheap on expiry day means the market thinks it will expire worthless. Usually it is right.\n\n**Avoid the last thirty minutes unless you have a specific plan.** Settlement mechanics and closing pressure dominate.\n\n**Size small.** Rapid price changes near expiry can magnify losses; smaller size does not remove that risk.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- Nifty weekly options expire **Tuesday**, Sensex weekly options **Thursday**. Bank Nifty has no weekly contracts; its options also have quarterly expiries.\n- Expiry-day price action is driven by option settlement, not by conviction.\n- Time decay and changes in implied volatility can reduce option premiums even when the underlying moves in your favour. Neither determines the result alone.\n- Treat expiry-day candles as noise unless you are specifically trading the expiry.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. F&O carries substantial risk and a large majority of retail derivatives traders lose money. Expiry schedules are set by SEBI and the exchanges and have changed more than once — always verify the current contract on the NSE or BSE website.*",
