    summary: "Follow the overnight relay that sets the starting point for India’s open.",
    demo: "chart",
    content: "# Gift Nifty and Global Cues: What Really Moves Our Open\n\n### *India opens at 9:15. The reasons it opens where it does were set overnight, elsewhere.*\n\nYou go to bed with Nifty at 25,100. You wake up and it opens at 24,880. Nothing happened in India. Everything happened while you were asleep.\n\nHere is the overnight chain that decides our morning.\n\n---\n\n## Gift Nifty: The Overnight Read\n\nGift Nifty — the Nifty derivative traded at **GIFT City, Gandhinagar**, on NSE International Exchange — is the closest thing we have to a live overnight Nifty. It trades across roughly 21 hours in two sessions, covering the Asian, European and US windows.\n\nIt replaced SGX Nifty, which shifted from Singapore to GIFT City in 2023.\n\n**How to use it:** before the open, check where Gift Nifty is trading relative to the previous Nifty close. That difference is the market's current best guess at our opening gap.\n\n**How not to use it:** it is an indicator, not a promise. Gift Nifty can be up 100 points and Nifty can open flat or reverse within minutes. Liquidity in overnight hours is thinner, so the signal is directionally useful and precisely unreliable.\n\n---\n\n## The Overnight Chain\n\nThink of the world as a relay race that ends at our open.\n\n**1. Previous day's US close (1:30 AM IST, roughly).** The biggest single input. S&P 500 and Nasdaq direction sets global risk appetite. A US tech sell-off can influence Indian IT stocks, but local news, valuations and other flows can outweigh it.\n\n**2. US futures overnight.** Still trading while we sleep. Often more relevant than the actual US close, because they reflect what happened *after* it.\n\n**3. Asian markets (open ~5:30–6:30 AM IST).** Japan, Korea, Hong Kong, China. They are the first to react to US moves and give an early read on whether the mood carried.\n\n**4. Crude oil.** India imports most of its oil. Sharp crude moves affect inflation expectations, the rupee, and directly hit OMCs, paints, aviation and tyres.\n\n**5. USD/INR and the Dollar Index.** A strengthening dollar generally pressures emerging markets. It also affects FII flows, IT earnings translation and import costs.\n\n**6. FII and DII activity.** The previous session's provisional figures, published by the exchanges, show whether foreign institutions were net buyers or sellers. Sustained FII selling can weigh on indices, but domestic flows and other factors can offset it.\n\n**7. Domestic overnight news.** Results announced after 3:30 PM, RBI actions, policy decisions, regulatory changes, large order wins.\n\n---\n\n## Sector-Level Transmission\n\nGlobal cues do not hit the market evenly. Knowing where they land is more useful than knowing the index direction.\n\n| Global input | Lands hardest on |\n|---|---|\n| US tech / Nasdaq | IT services, tech |\n| Crude oil up | OMCs, aviation, paints, tyres — negative |\n| Crude oil down | Same sectors — positive |\n| US bond yields up | Rate-sensitive: banks, NBFCs, realty |\n| Dollar strength | Importers negative, exporters positive |\n| Metal prices (LME) | Metals, mining |\n| Gold prices | Jewellery retail, gold financiers |\n\n---\n\n## The Discipline Around It\n\n**Check before the market opens, not after.** Review cues before opening and update them as new information arrives; their relevance can change during the session.\n\n**Do not trade the cue. Trade the reaction.** A weak global setup often produces a gap-down that is immediately bought. Gaps get filled frequently enough that fading them is a strategy in itself. The cue tells you the *starting point*, not the *direction of the day*.\n\n**Distinguish noise from regime.** One down night is weather. Three weeks of FII outflows and rising US yields is climate. A persistent pattern may warrant reviewing your assumptions, rather than automatically changing a position.\n\n**Do not over-anchor.** Domestic factors — monsoon, budget, RBI policy, domestic flows through SIPs — regularly override global cues entirely. India's large and growing domestic institutional flow has made our market less globally dependent than it was a decade ago.\n\n**Remember the gap risk.** This entire chain is exactly why an overnight position cannot be protected by a stop-loss. Global cues are the mechanism by which your stop gets skipped.\n\n---\n\n## A Simple Pre-Market Routine\n\nFive minutes, before 9:00 AM:\n\n1. Gift Nifty versus previous Nifty close — what is the implied gap?\n2. US close and current US futures.\n3. Asian markets right now.\n4. Crude and USD/INR.\n5. Previous day's FII/DII provisional numbers.\n6. Any overnight domestic news or results in stocks you hold.\n\nThat is the whole job. Then wait for the open and watch what the market does with it.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- **Gift Nifty** is the overnight proxy for Nifty — directional, not precise.\n- Our open is set by a relay: US close → US futures → Asia → commodities → currency → domestic news.\n- Global cues transmit **through sectors**, not uniformly.\n- The cue sets the opening level. **The reaction to the open is the actual trade.**\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Markets carry risk — please consult a SEBI-registered advisor before investing.*",
