    summary: "Know why surveillance measures can suddenly tighten margin, bands and liquidity.",
    demo: "rules",
    content: "# The ASM and GSM Lists: Why Your Stock Suddenly Can't Move\n\n### *Nothing was announced. Nothing was banned. Your stock was simply put on a shorter leash.*\n\nOne morning your smallcap needs 100% cash upfront. Intraday leverage is gone. The price band has tightened to 5%. Then to 2%. Then it trades only once a day.\n\nYou have not done anything wrong. Your stock has entered India's surveillance framework — and if you did not know it existed, you are about to learn the expensive way.\n\n---\n\n## What These Frameworks Are\n\nSEBI and the exchanges run surveillance systems that apply **extra friction** to stocks showing unusual behaviour. They are not accusations of fraud. They are speed bumps, applied by formula.\n\nThere are two main ones.\n\n**ASM — Additional Surveillance Measure.** Triggered largely by *price and volume behaviour*: unusual volatility, sharp price rise, high concentration of client activity, big changes in delivery percentage. A stock can be fundamentally sound and still land in ASM after a violent run.\n\n**GSM — Graded Surveillance Measure.** Triggered largely by *fundamentals*: weak financials, poor net worth, negative earnings, low book value — combined with a price that has run far ahead of any of it. GSM is the more serious of the two.\n\n---\n\n## What Actually Happens to You\n\nThe measures differ by framework and stage. This list is not a universal escalation sequence:\n\n| Restriction | What it means in practice |\n|---|---|\n| **100% margin** | No leverage. Full cash upfront to buy. |\n| **Tighter price band** | Band cut to 5% or lower, as applicable. Circuits hit far sooner. |\n| **Trade-for-trade settlement** | **No intraday.** Every buy must be delivered, every sell must come from your demat. |\n| **Periodic call auction** | The stock trades only in scheduled auction windows, not continuously. |\n| **Additional surveillance deposit** | Buyers must deposit an extra amount, held for months. |\n\nNSE GSM stages III and IV permit trading **once a week**, on Monday or the first trading day of the week, with a 100% additional surveillance deposit. Stage IV also restricts upward price movement. Periodic call auctions are a separate measure; check the notice for the security.\n\n---\n\n## Why This Traps People\n\n**Leverage vanishes overnight.** If you were carrying an intraday position with margin, you may be forced to square off or fund it fully. Positions get liquidated not because of price, but because of a rule change.\n\n**Liquidity evaporates.** Trade-for-trade and call auction status removes the day traders and arbitrageurs who were providing most of the volume. Spreads widen dramatically.\n\n**Tighter bands mean faster circuits.** A 5% or lower band means the stock can lock at lower circuit on an ordinary bad day. And once locked, there is no exit.\n\n**The combination is the killer.** Tight band plus no intraday plus thin liquidity means a falling GSM stock can go down for many consecutive sessions with holders completely unable to sell.\n\n---\n\n## The Signal Nobody Reads\n\nHere is the part worth internalising: **surveillance listing is often the market's clearest warning about a stock you were about to buy.**\n\nA stock that has quadrupled in eight months on no earnings and then enters GSM is telling you something specific. The exchange's formula noticed what excitement made you overlook.\n\nSimilarly, if a stock you already own gets added, that is a signal to reassess the position seriously — not to average down while telling yourself it is temporary.\n\n---\n\n## How to Protect Yourself\n\n**Check the list before you buy.** NSE and BSE publish current ASM and GSM lists on their websites, updated regularly. Most broker platforms flag it on the order screen. Look before, not after.\n\n**Treat surveillance status as a hard filter.** Many disciplined investors simply refuse to hold GSM-stage stocks. That is a perfectly rational rule.\n\n**Be extremely cautious with unsolicited smallcap tips.** Telegram-and-WhatsApp-driven stocks frequently end up in ASM or GSM, which is precisely the mechanism designed to catch that behaviour. If someone is urgently pushing you into an obscure counter, check the surveillance list first — it is a five-second sanity test.\n\n**Never build a large position in a name that could be band-restricted.** Size for the possibility that you cannot exit for several sessions.\n\n**Do not confuse ASM with guilt.** Good quality mid caps enter short-term ASM after strong rallies. That is volatility management, not a verdict on the company. Read which framework and which stage before panicking.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- **ASM** responds to price and volume behaviour; **GSM** responds to weak fundamentals plus a stretched price.\n- Restrictions depend on the framework and stage; check the current security-specific notice.\n- The real danger is **losing the ability to exit**, not the label itself.\n- The lists are published by NSE and BSE. **Check before you buy** — it is free information almost nobody uses.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Surveillance frameworks, stages and criteria are set by SEBI and the exchanges and are revised periodically — always verify the current list and applicable rules on the NSE or BSE website.*",
