    minutes: 7,
    summary: "The stop defines the risk. The risk defines the size. Decide both before entry.",
    demo: "expectancy",
    content: "# Position Sizing: The Boring Skill That Decides Everything\n\n### *You do not blow up an account by being wrong. You blow it up by being wrong big.*\n\nEveryone wants a better entry. Almost nobody works on size. Yet two traders can take identical trades — same stock, same entry, same stop — and one compounds steadily while the other is finished in six months. The only difference is how much they put on.\n\n---\n\n## The One Question\n\nBefore every trade, answer this:\n\n> **If this trade hits my stop, how much of my capital do I lose?**\n\nNot \"how many shares can I afford.\" Not \"how much margin do I have.\" **How much do I lose if I am wrong.**\n\nThat number should be small, fixed, and decided before you look at the chart.\n\n---\n\n## The 1% Rule\n\nA widely used starting point: **risk no more than 1% of your capital on a single trade.** Beginners are often better served by 0.5%.\n\nNote what this does *not* say. It does not say invest 1% of your capital. It says risk 1% — the amount you lose between entry and stop.\n\n**The formula:**\n\n```\nQuantity = (Capital × Risk %) ÷ (Entry price − Stop price)\n```\n\n**Worked example:**\n- Capital: ₹5,00,000\n- Risk per trade: 1% = ₹5,000\n- Entry: ₹850\n- Stop: ₹820\n- Risk per share: ₹30\n\n**₹5,000 ÷ ₹30 = 166.67; round down to 166 shares.** Position value ₹1,41,100 — about 28% of capital deployed. Planned loss at ₹820 is ₹4,980, just under 1%, before costs, slippage or a failed exit.\n\nChange the stop to ₹835 and the same 1% risk allows 333 shares. **The stop determines the size.** This is the mechanism most people have backwards: they pick a quantity first, then place a stop wherever it \"feels\" right.\n\n---\n\n## Why Small Risk Is Not Timid\n\nLosses compound against you asymmetrically. This is arithmetic, not psychology.\n\n| Drawdown | Gain needed to recover |\n|---|---|\n| 10% | 11% |\n| 25% | 33% |\n| 50% | **100%** |\n| 75% | **300%** |\n\nAt 1% risk per trade, ten consecutive losses — a bad but survivable streak — costs you roughly 10%. Recoverable.\n\nIf each loss is 10% of your remaining capital, ten consecutive losses leave about 35%. Recovering requires about a 187% gain, before costs. That recovery burden can also make disciplined decisions harder.\n\n**The first job of position sizing is to limit the damage from a losing streak.** Gaps, slippage and failed exits can still push losses beyond the plan. Every strategy has them.\n\n---\n\n## Adjusting for Volatility\n\nA fixed rupee stop makes no sense across different stocks. A 2% move in a large-cap bank is significant; in a volatile smallcap it is a normal hour.\n\nUse the stock's own volatility — commonly via **ATR (Average True Range)** — to set the stop, then let the formula size the position. Volatile stock, wider stop, smaller quantity. Stable stock, tighter stop, larger quantity. The same planned stop risk, before slippage, gaps and execution costs.\n\nThis is the whole trick: **risk stays constant, size varies.**\n\n---\n\n## The Indian Reality Check\n\n**Leverage is a size multiplier, not free money.** Intraday margin lets you take a position several times your capital. That does not change your risk rule — it just makes it easier to violate. Size from the risk formula, then check the margin. Never the reverse.\n\n**Overnight gaps break your stop.** Because our market closes, your stop cannot protect you across a gap open. If you hold overnight, assume your actual worst case is worse than your stop. Size accordingly.\n\n**Illiquid and circuit-prone stocks need smaller size.** If a stock can lock at lower circuit, your exit may not exist. In small caps, ask: can I sell this entire position in one day without moving the price? If not, the position is too big regardless of what the formula says.\n\n**F&O lot sizes are lumpy.** One Nifty lot may already exceed your 1% risk on a normal stop distance. If the smallest possible position violates your risk rule, **the correct answer is not to take the trade.** Skipping a trade that exceeds your risk limit avoids taking that oversized exposure.\n\n---\n\n## Portfolio-Level Limits\n\nIndividual trade sizing is not enough. Add two ceilings:\n\n**Total open risk.** If you have six positions each risking 1%, a correlated market drop can hit all six. Cap total open risk at 3–5% of capital.\n\n**Correlation.** Five bank stocks is one bet, not five. Size the theme, not the ticker.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- Decide **risk per trade** before anything else. 1% is a sensible ceiling; 0.5% while learning.\n- **The stop sets the size**, not the other way around.\n- Deep drawdowns require disproportionate gains to recover — that is the entire argument.\n- Adjust the stop for volatility, keep the risk constant.\n- If the minimum viable position breaks your risk rule, **skip the trade.**\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Markets carry risk — please consult a SEBI-registered advisor before investing.*",
