    content: "# The Real Cost of Overtrading\n\n### *Your P&L shows the trade. Your contract note shows the truth.*\n\nMost traders can tell you their win rate. Very few can tell you what they paid the system last year. That second number is often larger than the first one is worth — and published charges can be estimated, while spreads, slippage and future rate changes remain uncertain.\n\n---\n\n## What You Actually Pay\n\nEvery trade carries a stack of charges. Brokerage is usually the smallest one.\n\n**Brokerage** — typically flat per order (₹20 or a percentage, whichever is lower) at discount brokers; percentage-based at full-service brokers.\n\n**STT (Securities Transaction Tax)** — a government turnover tax, charged whether you win or lose.\n\n**Exchange transaction charges** — a percentage of turnover, set by NSE/BSE. Notably high per lakh of premium in options.\n\n**SEBI turnover fees** — small, but present.\n\n**Stamp duty** — state-level, on the buy side.\n\n**GST at 18%** — charged on brokerage and transaction charges.\n\n**DP charges** — a flat fee per scrip on the sell side of delivery trades. Not applicable to intraday or futures.\n\n---\n\n## STT: The Rates That Matter\n\n**These changed on 1 April 2026 under Budget 2026–27**, and a great deal of published content still quotes the old numbers.\n\n| Segment | Rate | Side |\n|---|---|---|\n| Equity delivery | 0.1% | Both buy and sell |\n| Equity intraday | 0.025% | Sell only |\n| **Futures** | **0.05%** *(was 0.02%)* | Sell only |\n| **Options (premium)** | **0.15%** *(was 0.10%)* | Sell only |\n| **Options (exercised)** | **0.15%** *(was 0.125%)* | On intrinsic value, buyer |\n\nThe futures rate rose by 150%. The stated intent was to add friction to short-tenor speculative derivatives trading.\n\n**What this does to a futures trader:** on a Nifty futures lot with a contract value around ₹13 lakh, STT per sell trade moves from roughly ₹260 to roughly ₹650. Where you previously needed about a 5-point move to cover STT, you now need roughly 12–13 points — before brokerage, exchange charges or GST.\n\nIf you built or backtested a strategy under the old rates, **it needs re-running.** A system that was marginally profitable at 0.02% may lose money at 0.05%.\n\n---\n\n## The Arithmetic That Ruins Accounts\n\nCosts are per trade. They scale with **frequency**, not with returns.\n\nConsider a trader with ₹2,00,000 capital taking 10 round-trip intraday trades per day.\n\n- 10 trades/day × ~21 trading days = **210 round trips per month**\n- At even ₹100 all-in per round trip, that is **₹21,000 per month**\n- **₹2,52,000 per year — 126% of starting capital**\n\nThe number per trade looked trivial. The annual number is ₹2,52,000 — 126% of the starting account, before trading gains or losses.\n\nNow consider the same person taking 20 trades a *month* instead of 10 a day. Costs drop to roughly ₹24,000 a year. Same capital, same skill, **a tenth of the friction.**\n\n**Frequency is the variable you control most easily and examine least often.**\n\n---\n\n## Why the Break-Even Point Is the Real Enemy\n\nCosts do not just subtract from profits. They **raise the bar your strategy must clear.**\n\nIf a round trip costs you 0.15% of position value, every trade starts 0.15% underwater. A strategy with a genuine 0.2% edge per trade keeps a quarter of it. A strategy with a 0.1% edge is now a losing strategy that feels like a winning one.\n\nThis is why so many traders have a decent win rate and a shrinking account. The edge was real. It just was not bigger than the friction.\n\n---\n\n## Options Traders: Read This Twice\n\nOptions can carry substantial trading costs, depending on turnover, liquidity and strategy:\n\n- STT at 0.15% on premium for sellers\n- Exchange transaction charges that are steep per lakh of premium\n- Brokerage on both legs of every spread — a four-leg strategy is four charges each way\n- Wide bid-ask spreads on non-liquid strikes, which is an invisible cost that never appears on your contract note\n- Exercised in-the-money options attracting STT on **intrinsic value**, which can be brutal on expiry\n\nCombine that with the fact that a large majority of retail derivatives traders lose money, and the friction is not a footnote — it is one contributor; these figures alone do not establish how much loss costs caused.\n\n---\n\n## What To Actually Do\n\n**Download your annual P&L and tax statement from your broker.** Every broker provides it. Find the total charges figure. Sit with it.\n\n**Calculate the ratio.** Total charges ÷ net profit. If charges exceed profits, you are working for the ecosystem.\n\n**Count your trades.** Then ask honestly how many were planned versus reactive. Boredom and revenge trades cost exactly the same as good ones.\n\n**Use a brokerage calculator before entering**, especially in F&O. Know your break-even before you know your target.\n\n**Widen your timeframe.** The simplest cost reduction available is fewer, better trades. Fewer trades can reduce charges, but do not automatically improve decisions or returns.\n\n**Re-test your system against the April 2026 rates** if it involves futures or options.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Recap\n\n- Costs are **per trade**, so overtrading is a fixed, guaranteed loss on top of market risk.\n- STT on futures rose to **0.05%** and on options premium to **0.15%** from 1 April 2026.\n- Friction raises your **break-even**, quietly converting small edges into losses.\n- Ten trades a day can cost more than your entire capital in a year.\n- Pull your annual statement. The number is already there — you just have not looked.\n\n---\n\n*Educational content only. This is not investment advice. Tax and charge rates are set by the government, SEBI and the exchanges and change frequently — verify current rates with your broker before relying on them.*",
